      "solution": "values = {\n    'I': 1,\n    'V': 5,\n    'X': 10,\n    'L': 50,\n    'C': 100,\n    'D': 500,\n    'M': 1000\n}\n\ns = input().strip()\nresult = 0\n\nfor i in range(len(s)):\n    if i + 1 < len(s) and values[s[i]] < values[s[i + 1]]:\n        result -= values[s[i]]\n    else:\n        result += values[s[i]]\n\nprint(result)\n",
      "statement": "Given a valid Roman numeral, convert it to its integer value.\n\nRoman numerals use seven symbols: I, V, X, L, C, D and M.\n\nUsually, symbols are written from largest to smallest. When a smaller symbol appears before a larger one, its value is subtracted instead.\n\nFor example, IV represents 4 and IX represents 9.",
